  "strategy": "# Approach and Rationale\nThe Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem seeks a step function h that minimizes the maximum overlap integral under given constraints. Our strategy leverages gradient-based optimization with careful constraint handling and periodic perturbations to escape local optima.\n\n# Mathematical Specification\nWe represent h as an array of n_points values in [0,1], satisfying \u222bh=1. The objective is C\u2085 = max_k (h \u2217 (1\u2212h))(k)dx, where \u2217 is cross-correlation. We formulate this as a constrained optimization problem:\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n\n- Constraint: \u2211h_i dx = 1\n- Objective: minimize max_k (correlate(h,1\u2212h,k))dx\n\n# Implementation Procedure\n1. **Initialization**:\n   - Use initial_h_values if available. Otherwise, create a uniform distribution.\n   - Clip and normalize to satisfy constraints.\n\n2. **Define Objective**:\n   Compute the overlap using np.correlate and return the maximum value scaled by dx.\n\n3. **Optimization Loop**:\n   - Use scipy.optimize.minimize with L-BFGS-B method.\n   - Apply periodic smoothing and perturbations to escape local minima.\n   - Project constraints after each update.\n\n4. **Constraint Handling**:\n   - Maintain \u2211h*dx=1 by rescaling after updates.\n   - Clip values to [0,1] to enforce bounds.\n\n5. **Termination**:\n   - Stop early if time budget is reached.\n   - Return best solution found.\n\n# Search Choices and Variations\n- Initial perturbation strength: 0.1\n- Smoothing window: 3-5\n- Perturbation frequency: every 10 iterations\n\n# Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Main cost: optimization and correlation computations.\n- Monitor runtime; fallback to initial solution if stalled.\n\n# Validation and Return\n- Ensure feasibility of final h.\n- Validate using the original evaluator.",
